  "body": "### Summary\n\nThe anonymizer's `--private` mode replaces MP bodies (`div#para…`) and subjects, but leaves two copies of MP text on composer and edit-form pages: the \"Vue Rapide de la discussion\" review (the cell after `td.messCase1bis`) and the `content_form` textarea (an edit's stored text, a quote prefill). Publishing such a page for a real conversation would leak private messages.\n\n### Facts\n\n- Capture run 9 (#337 batch 2, PR #400, `R95c`, `R95f`, `R95g`, and earlier `R47b`, `R47c`, `R43c`): every one is a page of the operator's fixture threads (`fixture-test-N`), so the corpus holds no leak today. On those pages the review keeps the rendered posts and `content_form` the source.\n- `anonymize.py check --private` does not flag either position, so `publish.py` would accept a real thread's composer.\n- The MPStorage edit form (R60b) already gets a dedicated check of `content_form` and of the review (`mpstorage_form_copies`): the same positions, handled for one thread only.\n\n### Change\n\nIn `tooling/fixtures/anonymize.py`, in private mode: replace the review cell's content and `content_form` by the private placeholder unless the page's thread is a fixture thread (`fixture-test-N` in the hidden or visible `sujet`) or the MPStorage thread (existing check); add both positions to `check --private` (both verifiers). Synthetic tests in `tooling/fixtures/tests/` (a real-looking thread composer leaks without the change). Republishing the existing fixture-thread pages must leave them unchanged.\n\nRefs #172, #334\n",
